{
  "requirement": "For each {term}`requirements.sdk.graph.search.hit` in a text or structural search, locate the start of the match in the outline of the **same snapshot**. Select the innermost item containing that position. The item's project-relative file path and selector form its `OutlineId`. When no smaller item contains the position, use the file root (empty selector).",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.graph.search.enclosing_item`.\n\n**Implementation shape.** Text and structural search both go through `SourceProject::find_hits` in `sources/hits.rs`. It calls `file.at_offset(range.start)` and builds the `OutlineId` from the path and the item's selector. Each obligation is therefore reached by both search kinds, and one set of tests can cover it.\n\n**Obligations and tests**\n- **Innermost item.**\n  - `separated_hit_windows_preserve_the_function_declaration` searches a hit nested in `impl Runner { fn run }`. It asserts the node is `runner.rs#impl[Runner]/fn[run]`, so the innermost item is chosen over the enclosing impl.\n  - `structural_tags_select_only_the_original_enclosing_item` checks structural hits. Two `call()` hits resolve to `code.rs#fn[one]` and `code.rs#fn[two]` with the exact spans.\n- **Start of the match decides the item.** `matches_crossing_item_boundaries_belong_to_the_item_at_the_start` uses `fn a() {}   fn b() {}` with three patterns:\n  - A match starting in the whitespace between the two items gives the root, `pair.rs#`.\n  - A match starting at `}` of `a` and running into `b` gives `fn[a]`.\n  - A match starting at `fn b` gives `fn[b]`.\n  This tests the \"start of the match\" rule and the boundary cases.\n- **Root fallback.** `root_hits_clip_context_to_utf8_boundaries` finds hits that no item contains, in the fragments `needle` and `éhit猫`. It asserts the node is `fragment.rs#` (empty selector), and it checks that the node content is the whole source.\n- **Same snapshot.**\n  - Both the structural test and the separated-hit test overwrite the file after the first search and then search again.\n  - They still expect the original items, `fn[one]`, `fn[two]` and `impl[Runner]/fn[run]`.\n  - An implementation that re-read a changed file for the outline would produce a different item or the root. These assertions rule that out.\n\n**Untested corners.** No test covers a structural hit nested inside a container or a structural hit that falls at the root. Both go through the same `find_hits` path, which the text-search tests exercise. This is not a distinct obligation, so I do not count it as a gap.\n\nI found no unacknowledged gaps.",
